00:02:03.380 --> 00:02:13.820
 David Asher is an organic farmer, a farmstead cheesemaker, and a cheese educator based in the Gulf Islands of British Columbia, Canada.

00:02:14.370 --> 00:02:19.330
 He's a guerrilla cheesemaker, so he doesn't make cheese according to the standard industrial philosophies.

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:22.970
 He explores traditionally cultured, non-corporate methods of cheesemaking.

00:02:23.850 --> 00:02:28.530
 David offers cheese outreach to communities near and far with the Black Sheep School of Cheesemaking.

00:02:28.530 --> 00:02:34.790
 Through these workshops, in partnership with food sovereignty-minded organizations, he shares his distinct cheesemaking style.

00:02:35.130 --> 00:02:41.590
 His workshops teach a cheesemaking method that's natural, DIY, and well-suited to the home kitchen or artisanal production.

00:02:42.070 --> 00:02:45.070
 He's the author of the book, The Art of Natural Cheesemaking.

00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:46.440
 You can find it anywhere.

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:54.670
 In this episode, David talks with me about raw milk, kefir cultures, and how diversity makes cheese and food systems resilient.

00:02:55.340 --> 00:03:03.480
 So I'm here today with David Asher, and I just spent all day with you at a cheesemaking workshop here in Healdsburg in Sonoma County.

00:03:03.660 --> 00:03:04.800
 I learned a ton.

00:03:04.940 --> 00:03:12.420
 There were some things like making yogurt that I've tried before, but a lot of cheesemaking that I had no idea how it was done.

00:03:12.420 --> 00:03:19.900
 And also that I think you've developed some of these techniques, these unique ways of making cheese at home.

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:25.140
 I just wanted to start off asking you about what's important about making cheese at home.

00:03:26.420 --> 00:03:33.600
 Making cheese at home is all about taking back our culture, literally taking back the culture of our food.

00:03:34.880 --> 00:03:42.060
 Cheese is a very cultured food and traditionally invoked all these wonderful bacterial cultures that people kept at home

00:03:42.060 --> 00:03:48.920
 through letting their raw milk sour, through invoking the natural microorganisms present in raw milk.

00:03:49.660 --> 00:03:55.680
 This cultured food prepared at home was traditionally a huge part of our culture,

00:03:55.680 --> 00:04:02.400
 and cultures around the world continue to make dairy at home as part of the regular part of their routines.

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:11.980
 Folks in France, in the Middle East, across Africa, across Southern America, make cheese at home the same way we cook.

00:04:12.080 --> 00:04:15.480
 It fits into the rhythms of daily life.

00:04:15.900 --> 00:04:20.850
 But here in North America, we're kind of separated from that.

00:04:21.190 --> 00:04:24.370
 We're a couple of generations removed from traditional cheesemaking.

00:04:24.370 --> 00:04:28.510
 We haven't been making it at home because we're intimidated by the idea of working with dairy.

00:04:29.030 --> 00:04:31.990
 We're afraid of letting raw milk sit out and clabber.

00:04:32.490 --> 00:04:38.930
 We're conditioned to fear for our dairy that raw milk is a source of illness and fear,

00:04:39.570 --> 00:04:42.270
 and we need to pasteurize and sterilize.

00:04:42.870 --> 00:04:44.990
 But none of that is true.

00:04:45.170 --> 00:04:45.950
 None of that is real.

00:04:46.590 --> 00:04:51.750
 It's all just this made-up way that we've created in North America

00:04:51.750 --> 00:05:01.390
 that has no bearing on what milk really is.

00:05:01.610 --> 00:05:08.210
 And milk is a beautiful, healthful substance when it comes from well-raised, healthy, pastured animals.

00:05:08.810 --> 00:05:15.170
 When we get milk from a good source, we can be assured of its healthfulness and its goodness.

00:05:15.350 --> 00:05:16.770
 We don't need to pasteurize it.

00:05:17.170 --> 00:05:20.670
 In order to work with it, we can make cheese with it safely at home.

00:05:20.870 --> 00:05:25.470
 We can take back our cheesemaking from the corporate forces that have really taken it away from us

00:05:25.470 --> 00:05:29.710
 and have done all sorts of nasty things with this most favorite food of ours,

00:05:30.130 --> 00:05:33.130
 using genetically modified rennets to coagulate the curd,

00:05:33.610 --> 00:05:39.070
 using freeze-dried corporate cultures raised in distant laboratories in Denmark.

00:05:40.450 --> 00:05:45.050
 We have lost so much in our cheesemaking in North America.

00:05:45.230 --> 00:05:48.990
 Even our artisanal cheesemakers are doing things in an industrial way.

00:05:49.110 --> 00:05:53.550
 Home cheesemakers who use the standard approaches are doing things according to methods

00:05:53.550 --> 00:05:56.630
 that they may not feel so happy about if they knew the full story.

00:05:57.470 --> 00:06:00.270
 And traditional cheesemaking methods have essentially been lost,

00:06:00.430 --> 00:06:03.870
 and I'm really working to revitalize those methods

00:06:03.870 --> 00:06:07.960
 and encourage people to take back these techniques

00:06:07.960 --> 00:06:10.400
 that were once a part of our daily lives but that have been lost

00:06:10.400 --> 00:06:12.860
 to reclaim these traditional cultures

00:06:12.860 --> 00:06:16.940
 that could form an important part of our lives and of our diets.

00:06:17.910 --> 00:06:18.050
 Awesome.

00:06:18.250 --> 00:06:19.490
 Well, let's talk.

00:06:20.850 --> 00:06:26.170
 I guess one of the things that I haven't heard is your story of how you started making cheese.

00:06:26.470 --> 00:06:29.350
 I heard it coming in and out of the workshop a little bit.

00:06:29.570 --> 00:06:31.810
 I didn't talk about it too much in class today.

00:06:32.210 --> 00:06:34.170
 But I had no idea I would be a cheesemaker.

00:06:34.910 --> 00:06:36.850
 A few cheesemakers in North America do.

00:06:36.990 --> 00:06:40.010
 It's something that we fall in love with later in life.

00:06:42.050 --> 00:06:43.070
 Something we discover.

00:06:43.550 --> 00:06:47.670
 And in my case, I discovered cheesemaking entirely by chance

00:06:47.670 --> 00:06:51.830
 by visiting a farmer one day who was making cheese at home

00:06:51.830 --> 00:06:55.350
 with raw milk from her own goats

00:06:55.350 --> 00:06:59.690
 and making these inspired, beautiful cheeses that she was creating in her home

00:06:59.690 --> 00:07:01.850
 and aging in like a broken down old refrigerator

00:07:01.850 --> 00:07:04.470
 that she'd modified the thermostat on to create the right conditions

00:07:04.470 --> 00:07:07.650
 and to age these beautiful rounds of moldy blue cheeses.

00:07:08.030 --> 00:07:13.270
 And I visited this cheesemaker on a cycling trip

00:07:13.270 --> 00:07:16.950
 when I was going out exploring opportunities in farming,

00:07:17.130 --> 00:07:20.410
 looking to decide if it was a livelihood that I was interested in.

00:07:20.470 --> 00:07:23.270
 And I came across this farmer in a beautiful farm

00:07:23.270 --> 00:07:24.630
 and her amazing cheeses.

00:07:24.630 --> 00:07:28.490
 And after tasting her beautiful cheeses

00:07:28.490 --> 00:07:32.990
 and seeing how much her love transferred from her to the animals to her cheeses,

00:07:33.650 --> 00:07:37.360
 I was really moved by the methods

00:07:37.360 --> 00:07:41.600
 and by the fact that she just made this amazing food in her own home

00:07:41.600 --> 00:07:43.680
 without any specialized tools or equipment.

00:07:44.700 --> 00:07:47.780
 And it seemed totally, entirely possible.

00:07:48.960 --> 00:07:51.400
 And I took that spark with me back home

00:07:51.400 --> 00:07:54.500
 and decided to try my hand at making cheese on my own.

00:07:54.780 --> 00:07:57.440
 I was really into food in all ways at the time.

00:07:57.580 --> 00:08:01.440
 I was really excited about gardening, about growing, about baking, about fermenting,

00:08:02.500 --> 00:08:03.780
 about cooking.

00:08:04.280 --> 00:08:06.240
 And cheesemaking just fit right in.

00:08:06.480 --> 00:08:09.860
 And it started occupying a lot more of my interest

00:08:09.860 --> 00:08:11.080
 and a lot more of my energy.

00:08:11.820 --> 00:08:15.440
 I really got amazed by the transformation that milk goes through.

00:08:15.500 --> 00:08:19.220
 It's one of the most remarkable foods that's out there.

00:08:19.360 --> 00:08:21.320
 It's totally adaptable

00:08:21.320 --> 00:08:28.220
 and can make these transformations into all sorts of weird forms

00:08:28.220 --> 00:08:30.700
 and remarkable flavors and textures and colors.

00:08:31.060 --> 00:08:34.780
 And the more I explored it, the more addicted I became.

00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:39.230
 And I was living in a city at the time,

00:08:39.330 --> 00:08:41.330
 you know, gardening, being involved in community farms.

00:08:41.950 --> 00:08:44.630
 But it was getting involved in cheesemaking

00:08:44.630 --> 00:08:48.250
 that encouraged me to go out and really seriously farm

00:08:48.250 --> 00:08:52.730
 because I wanted to get access to good milk to use to make cheeses with.

00:08:52.870 --> 00:08:54.610
 And at the time when I was living in the city,

00:08:54.650 --> 00:08:56.270
 it was really difficult, really challenging

00:08:56.270 --> 00:09:00.410
 to gain access to good, you know, wholesome, pastured, raw milk.

00:09:00.930 --> 00:09:03.850
 And so I ended up leaving the city and doing an apprenticeship

00:09:03.850 --> 00:09:08.470
 and getting more and more involved in dairying and cheesemaking.

00:09:08.530 --> 00:09:12.610
 You kind of have to know somebody to get your hands on raw milk, right?

00:09:12.690 --> 00:09:13.090
 You do.

00:09:13.350 --> 00:09:15.690
 And, you know, my first taste of raw milk

00:09:15.690 --> 00:09:19.030
 came from a pair of raw milk farmers

00:09:19.030 --> 00:09:22.970
 on a neighboring island to the island that I was apprenticing on.

00:09:23.330 --> 00:09:28.250
 And they were Episcopalian priests who would come and minister on our island.

00:09:28.310 --> 00:09:32.920
 And when they came over by speedboat to deliver their sermon,

00:09:33.320 --> 00:09:38.120
 they'd also deliver raw milk from their small herd of goats.

00:09:38.540 --> 00:09:42.140
 And they delivered it in these, you know, two liter plastic pop bottles

00:09:42.140 --> 00:09:44.800
 that they obviously, you know, reused, recycled, recovered.

00:09:45.380 --> 00:09:52.250
 And it, you know, it was really something else to me.

00:09:53.430 --> 00:09:57.830
 It was like a, you know, I didn't get my revelation from going to their sermons.

00:09:57.990 --> 00:10:01.290
 I got my revelation from seeing these clergy, you know,

00:10:01.790 --> 00:10:04.730
 selling an illicit substance in Canada,

00:10:04.810 --> 00:10:08.050
 the sale and distribution of raw milk to the public is illegal.

00:10:08.050 --> 00:10:12.130
 And the very fact that, you know, these priests were dealing with raw milk

00:10:12.130 --> 00:10:16.550
 just totally blew my mind and made me realize that, you know,

00:10:16.590 --> 00:10:20.890
 raw milk is something really to worship.

00:10:22.210 --> 00:10:25.550
 And, you know, they realized it was something that was really important

00:10:25.550 --> 00:10:29.150
 for their community and, you know, a place of influence that they were.

00:10:29.270 --> 00:10:34.710
 They were really eager to encourage people to gain access to this food

00:10:34.710 --> 00:10:36.890
 that was essentially being, you know, this amazing food

00:10:36.890 --> 00:10:39.670
 that was essentially being kept out of our reach.

00:10:41.050 --> 00:10:44.470
 It's funny that priests introduce you to raw milk

00:10:44.470 --> 00:10:47.590
 because one of the places that we have gotten raw milk around here

00:10:47.590 --> 00:10:50.890
 in Sonoma County is from a Hindu ashram

00:10:50.890 --> 00:10:55.750
 that is one of the main providers of raw milk around here.

00:10:57.460 --> 00:11:02.410
 So, yeah, let's talk a little bit about kefir or kaffir

00:11:02.410 --> 00:11:04.070
 or however you pronounce it.

00:11:04.210 --> 00:11:04.850
 It doesn't matter.

00:11:05.130 --> 00:11:06.230
 It's the same culture.

00:11:07.710 --> 00:11:09.250
 Kiefer, kefir, it doesn't matter.

00:11:09.970 --> 00:11:14.710
 Was that, did you just cover that before you started making cheese or after?

00:11:14.990 --> 00:11:16.950
 It's at the center of your cheesemaking process.

00:11:17.170 --> 00:11:17.330
 Yeah.

00:11:17.670 --> 00:11:21.210
 Kefir or kefir forms the basis of my cheesemaking process.

00:11:21.370 --> 00:11:23.970
 It is the culture that has inspired my cheesemaking.

00:11:24.350 --> 00:11:26.950
 And it is an unreal culture.

00:11:27.090 --> 00:11:29.850
 It's almost hard to believe that this thing exists.

00:11:30.230 --> 00:11:33.910
 If you have a basic understanding of microbiology,

00:11:33.990 --> 00:11:40.660
 it seems so counterintuitive that this being could ever possibly be.

00:11:41.380 --> 00:11:47.270
 It's not, I guess, a single species of microorganisms in this kefir culture,

00:11:47.350 --> 00:11:51.050
 but hundreds of different species of microorganisms living together in this community,

00:11:51.570 --> 00:11:55.310
 this symbiotic community of bacteria and yeast, this scoby, as it's called.

00:11:56.330 --> 00:12:04.500
 It is like a complex, multicellular, multi-species microorganism that thrives simply on milk.

00:12:04.820 --> 00:12:06.880
 And you feed it milk, and the culture is happy.

00:12:07.620 --> 00:12:12.040
 And it feeds, and the culture transforms that milk into kefir,

00:12:12.740 --> 00:12:17.400
 which is a remarkably flavorful, probiotic, effervescent beverage.

00:12:17.880 --> 00:12:21.660
 You strain the kefir grains out of that fermented kefir,

00:12:21.780 --> 00:12:24.740
 you put them into some fresh milk, and they ferment that fresh milk into more kefir.

00:12:24.840 --> 00:12:27.300
 And you can continue that on essentially forever.

00:12:27.820 --> 00:12:29.880
 And this culture has been cultured forever.

00:12:30.480 --> 00:12:35.400
 Literally 5,000 or 6,000 years, this culture has been passed down from generation to generation.

00:12:35.400 --> 00:12:39.720
 People have valued it for, you know, and venerated it for many, many different reasons.

00:12:40.180 --> 00:12:42.540
 And the culture has, you know, continued to be kept to this day.

00:12:43.140 --> 00:12:46.280
 And I've been keeping it for a decade or so.

00:12:46.400 --> 00:12:48.380
 Really, I've been charmed by it.

00:12:48.440 --> 00:12:51.260
 I think that the culture has been slowly taken over in my brain.

00:12:52.240 --> 00:12:59.600
 And I've been taking this culture and sharing it with folks all over.

00:12:59.660 --> 00:13:02.500
 Every time I give a workshop, I share kefir grains with everybody who's interested today.

00:13:02.500 --> 00:13:05.540
 I share, you know, kefir grains with 13 or 14 interested students,

00:13:05.720 --> 00:13:07.720
 and I do that almost, you know, twice a week.

00:13:08.600 --> 00:13:13.380
 And the kefir culture is so easy to keep at home, so easy to care for.

00:13:13.620 --> 00:13:18.040
 And the complex diversity of microorganisms living in it

00:13:18.040 --> 00:13:23.140
 allow the kefir culture to serve as a sort of universal cheese-making starter culture

00:13:23.140 --> 00:13:25.560
 for preparing almost any style of cheese,

00:13:25.680 --> 00:13:30.780
 from camembert to brie to blue cheese to Alpine-style cheeses, cheddar,

00:13:32.160 --> 00:13:35.600
 even creme fraiche, cultured butter, and yogurt can be made with it all.

00:13:36.360 --> 00:13:36.600
 Amazing.

00:13:36.860 --> 00:13:46.080
 And this isn't a, you know, there's not a lot of cheesemakers that make cheese starting with that culture.

00:13:46.300 --> 00:13:48.940
 No, no commercial cheesemakers use it.

00:13:49.000 --> 00:13:52.360
 There might be one or two in North America who use it in their cheesemaking operation.

00:13:52.760 --> 00:13:57.300
 But by and large, cheesemakers who make their cheese use these packages of freeze-dried starter culture,

00:13:57.400 --> 00:13:59.660
 like packaged yeast you might buy in the grocery store.

00:14:00.220 --> 00:14:02.740
 But these packaged freeze-dried starter cultures, by and large,

00:14:02.820 --> 00:14:09.220
 come from a company called Danisco that's a subsidiary of DuPont, now Dow DuPont.

00:14:09.440 --> 00:14:14.100
 And, you know, purchasing these packaged starter cultures is essentially, you know,

00:14:14.100 --> 00:14:17.020
 supporting the corporate interests that are taking, you know,

00:14:17.080 --> 00:14:20.940
 the power away from farmers and artisans all across North America.

00:14:21.060 --> 00:14:25.780
 So it's totally counterproductive to be, you know, making cheese using these, you know,

00:14:25.840 --> 00:14:27.640
 these purchased corporate cultures.

00:14:28.160 --> 00:14:32.810
 Right, and that's what I bought online when I had it, when I was raising goats, right,

00:14:32.890 --> 00:14:33.290
 and making...

00:14:33.290 --> 00:14:33.790
 We'll sell you today.

00:14:33.990 --> 00:14:34.750
 Yeah, totally.

00:14:35.630 --> 00:14:41.530
 So one difference is that, you know, it's an input that you have to buy from a corporate source.

00:14:41.630 --> 00:14:46.660
 And another one is the huge difference in diversity of organisms.

00:14:47.180 --> 00:14:54.610
 I mean, Chelsea and I do research about biodiversity and how that helps farmers in southern Mexico

00:14:54.610 --> 00:14:57.890
 get through hard times and hurricanes and droughts and that kind of thing.

00:14:58.750 --> 00:15:01.970
 So I was thinking about when you were talking about the biodiversity and keepers

00:15:01.970 --> 00:15:05.190
 and how resilient that makes the cheesemaking process,

00:15:05.190 --> 00:15:08.210
 so I was thinking a lot about the corollaries between those things.

00:15:08.430 --> 00:15:09.690
 Right, it fits right in.

00:15:09.830 --> 00:15:14.170
 Like, you know, my experiences with kefir are inspired by, you know,

00:15:14.210 --> 00:15:17.610
 the ecological approach to farming that I take and, you know, vice versa.

00:15:18.590 --> 00:15:25.250
 Like, seeing the diversity of kefir and recognizing that its strength really brings together

00:15:25.250 --> 00:15:27.290
 a lot of different philosophies in a lot of different areas,

00:15:27.290 --> 00:15:34.430
 and it's validating and amazing to see that this culture works in such a way

00:15:34.430 --> 00:15:37.630
 that the diversity is its strength and allows it to work in the way that it does.

00:15:38.070 --> 00:15:46.410
 I made a note during your workshop today that you talk about kefir like almost like this mythical creature.

00:15:46.610 --> 00:15:48.270
 Its origin is a little bit unknown.

00:15:48.530 --> 00:15:49.290
 Yeah, right?

00:15:49.790 --> 00:15:54.490
 And at the same time, it's this really simple, low-tech way of making food.

00:15:54.620 --> 00:16:00.960
 Yeah, so the culture is something that you can so easily keep.

00:16:01.120 --> 00:16:02.260
 You know, you feed it milk every day.

00:16:02.340 --> 00:16:03.560
 It feeds you back kefir.

00:16:03.780 --> 00:16:06.040
 It's like a sourdough starter culture just for dairy.

00:16:06.400 --> 00:16:09.820
 It forms these little granules that are so easy to care for,

00:16:09.900 --> 00:16:12.420
 that are kind of cute and endearing and fun to play with.

00:16:13.120 --> 00:16:21.040
 And, you know, the culture itself is so easy to use as a cheesemaking starter culture and so adaptable,

00:16:21.120 --> 00:16:22.620
 it's almost hard to believe that it's real.

00:16:22.740 --> 00:16:27.440
 Because cheesemakers who make cheese with these package starter cultures have to buy dozens of different packages

00:16:27.440 --> 00:16:29.480
 for making dozens of different styles of cheese.

00:16:30.040 --> 00:16:32.380
 They cannot, you know, keep the starter cultures.

00:16:32.500 --> 00:16:34.960
 They have to buy new ones every single time they make cheese.

00:16:35.380 --> 00:16:36.980
 They cannot propagate them themselves.

00:16:37.970 --> 00:16:40.410
 They have to, you know, create perfectly sterile conditions

00:16:40.410 --> 00:16:44.590
 in order for the cultures that they purchase from these packages to propagate.

00:16:44.750 --> 00:16:50.050
 Because if they're not perfectly sterile, the cultures will be invaded by unwanted raw milk microorganisms

00:16:50.050 --> 00:16:53.430
 and bacterial phages that will wipe out the bacterial populations.

00:16:54.150 --> 00:16:57.110
 But this kefir culture seems to be resistant to all of that.

00:16:57.110 --> 00:17:01.210
 Like the culture itself, you can use it for making any different style of cheese

00:17:01.210 --> 00:17:06.490
 because it has in it all these different strains of microorganism that cheesemakers buy in packages.

00:17:07.010 --> 00:17:09.250
 You know, the culture is infinitely propagable.

00:17:09.350 --> 00:17:11.690
 You can keep it forever and pass it on to your grandkids,

00:17:11.750 --> 00:17:13.390
 and they can keep it forever and do the same.

00:17:14.100 --> 00:17:18.660
 Whereas these purchase package starter cultures are, you know, you can't use them more than once.

00:17:18.720 --> 00:17:22.720
 You cannot reuse the cultures because these sensitive laboratory-based cultures

00:17:22.720 --> 00:17:25.660
 just aren't totally non-propagable, just like hybrid seeds.

00:17:27.100 --> 00:17:36.100
 And the cultures, the kefir culture, you know, it's a culture that you can, like, hold and feel in your hand.

00:17:36.160 --> 00:17:39.840
 And it's almost hard to believe that that's actually, like, microorganisms in that thing.

00:17:39.920 --> 00:17:43.720
 That thing is alive and full of life and full of possibility.

00:17:44.300 --> 00:17:45.140
 It's really beautiful.

00:17:45.480 --> 00:17:48.500
 And, yeah, mythical and sort of almost hard to believe.

00:17:48.500 --> 00:17:50.050
 Awesome.

00:17:51.440 --> 00:18:00.530
 It seems like there's this ethic of sharing knowledge about food and sharing cultures that are part of –

00:18:00.530 --> 00:18:04.450
 definitely I've done sourdough before, and there's this really strong thing about, like,

00:18:04.720 --> 00:18:07.760
 oh, you make sourdough dough, like, we shouldn't talk, you know.

00:18:08.460 --> 00:18:09.580
 Kombucha's like that.

00:18:10.360 --> 00:18:13.480
 Seeds are like that all over the world that I've been in.

00:18:13.480 --> 00:18:15.380
 Oh, in many places, right?

00:18:15.480 --> 00:18:22.820
 Like, the culture of our seed sharing has been severely hit by corporate involvement in our seed-saving programs

00:18:22.820 --> 00:18:25.960
 and, you know, created seed varieties that we cannot keep, that we have to, you know,

00:18:25.980 --> 00:18:29.080
 keep on buying year after year after year to keep on planting.

00:18:29.760 --> 00:18:32.900
 And that was, you know, not always the way that, you know,

00:18:32.900 --> 00:18:37.260
 there used to be seed fairs where people would come together and share all the different seed varieties

00:18:37.260 --> 00:18:38.820
 that would have saved over the year.

00:18:39.000 --> 00:18:41.200
 And they would have exchanged varieties with other farmers.

00:18:41.360 --> 00:18:45.680
 They would have shared the, you know, the projects that they were working on,

00:18:45.720 --> 00:18:51.220
 all these interesting new varieties that they were developing that were persistent to illnesses that were rampant.

00:18:51.220 --> 00:18:54.690
 And, you know, like the culture around fermented foods is very similar.

00:18:54.870 --> 00:19:00.430
 People come together for culture swaps where they share cultures and they share stories and experiences

00:19:00.430 --> 00:19:02.370
 and encourage each other all around the table.

00:19:03.350 --> 00:19:06.560
 And, you know, that's not the way it is in cheesemaking.

00:19:06.740 --> 00:19:08.760
 Cheesemakers are very protective of their methods.

00:19:09.020 --> 00:19:14.760
 They, the cultures have been totally corporatized and you're not, you just can't share them with one another.

00:19:15.080 --> 00:19:18.300
 But, you know, once upon a time, that's, that's the way it was.

00:19:19.860 --> 00:19:21.760
 The culture, you know, freely flowed.

00:19:21.760 --> 00:19:25.540
 People kept kefir grains and shared them with their family, their friends, their neighbors.

00:19:26.940 --> 00:19:32.950
 These kefir cultures, you know, that I keep and they use in my cheesemaking have been kept and passed down from generation to generation.

00:19:33.130 --> 00:19:33.870
 They were never hoarded.

00:19:34.810 --> 00:19:36.270
 They're never privatized.

00:19:36.770 --> 00:19:41.730
 And the culture is, you know, totally approachable to anybody who's interested in keeping it.

00:19:45.340 --> 00:19:55.140
 One of the things about that I liked during your, your workshop was this idea that you did a lot of experimenting to figure out how to make these cheeses.

00:19:55.340 --> 00:20:05.420
 And like, there's a lot of like, of new that you were trying to figure out, but that was also infused with this idea, like looking back at some, at some, um, some traditional ways of doing things.

00:20:05.660 --> 00:20:15.270
 Um, could you, there was one story I really liked about, about trying, hearing this old Georgian story about how to clobber milk.

00:20:15.430 --> 00:20:16.230
 Can you tell that story?

00:20:16.730 --> 00:20:21.060
 Um, so I, I was searching for traditional methods of making yogurt.

00:20:21.330 --> 00:20:26.060
 Um, and the way typically people make yogurt is they, in order to make yogurt, you need yogurt.

00:20:26.180 --> 00:20:33.510
 You need to, um, get a scoop full of yogurt, full of live active yogurt cultures and use that as a starter culture for making, you know, making more yogurt.

00:20:33.850 --> 00:20:46.470
 And typically yogurt makers today, um, it wasn't always this way, but typically yogurt makers today, um, will, um, either buy commercial yogurt they bought at the grocery store in order to make their own yogurt.

00:20:46.950 --> 00:20:53.780
 Um, and, uh, or they'll purchase freeze dried package cultures from cheese making supply shops and use those starter cultures for making their yogurt.

00:20:54.080 --> 00:20:59.380
 Uh, but once upon a time people kept, you know, people propagated, uh, the culture of their yogurt making.

00:20:59.380 --> 00:21:00.820
 Uh, they kept these cultures at home.

00:21:00.920 --> 00:21:02.480
 They used them over and over again.

00:21:02.480 --> 00:21:11.150
 And in using the cultures over and over again, they assured, uh, the health and viability of their, of their culture, uh, just by being involved with it.

00:21:11.580 --> 00:21:22.020
 Um, and you know, I, I, in my explorations of, of cheese making, I always, you know, question the origin story.

00:21:22.020 --> 00:21:24.640
 Like I always, I always go there for, for inspiration.

00:21:24.640 --> 00:21:34.650
 Like, uh, my, uh, inspiration for discovering blue cheese came from, you know, discovering how to propagate the blue cheese fungus came from, uh, uh, uh,

00:21:34.940 --> 00:21:40.370
 hearing about traditional, the traditional origin story of where blue, you know, blue cheeses came from.

00:21:41.520 --> 00:21:47.010
 Um, and in the case of yogurt, um, I wondered where the original yogurt culture came from.

00:21:47.050 --> 00:21:50.640
 Like, where did the first yogurt makers get their yogurt starter culture come from?

00:21:50.760 --> 00:21:53.160
 Because yogurt obviously didn't, you know, start as yogurt.

00:21:53.220 --> 00:21:54.480
 It must have started as something else.

00:21:55.000 --> 00:21:56.240
 Uh, must have come from somewhere.

00:21:56.380 --> 00:21:58.300
 The culture for yogurt must have come from somewhere else.

00:21:58.300 --> 00:22:01.500
 Because in the process of making yogurt, you have to cook milk.

00:22:01.560 --> 00:22:08.540
 And you're therefore pasteurize the milk before you add back the culture to the cooked milk in order for the yogurt to take on the qualities that it needs.

00:22:08.540 --> 00:22:14.460
 And so somewhere down the line, this yogurt, original yogurt starter culture must have come from somewhere else other than yogurt.

00:22:14.980 --> 00:22:21.980
 And there's all these stories that are out there about the origin of yogurt culture, um, uh, from different places around the world.

00:22:21.980 --> 00:22:33.840
 There's stories from, uh, Finland and Norway about this particular plant called the butterwort that people would add to their, that people would place in their milk that would, uh, cause the milk to naturally curdle.

00:22:33.980 --> 00:22:40.200
 And then that curdled milk would be used as a starter culture for making cheese, but I didn't have any butterwort growing in my bio region.

00:22:40.200 --> 00:22:42.000
 So I couldn't use that as a starter culture.

00:22:42.570 --> 00:22:50.080
 Uh, uh, I also spoke to a friend who had spent a lot of time in Georgia, the country, not the state.

00:22:50.080 --> 00:22:54.020
 And Georgia is at the heart of yogurt making regions of Eastern Europe.

00:22:54.700 --> 00:22:59.120
 And she told me that in Georgia, they have a sort of origin story about where yogurt came from.

00:22:59.160 --> 00:23:15.750
 And the story is that, uh, the original yogurt cultures came from milk that was left out in the pasture on a full moon night to coagulate as a result of the forces of the, of the, of the universe, like concentrating in, in this, in this, in this milk.

00:23:15.750 --> 00:23:25.370
 Um, and, um, that thickened milk, uh, was then used as a starter culture for making yogurt.

00:23:25.750 --> 00:23:31.720
 Um, and the yogurt thickened up beautifully and, um, I was inspired by that story and decided to try it.

00:23:31.720 --> 00:23:39.760
 So I took a little bit of my raw milk and I left it out on, you know, my animal's pasture on a full moon night, but it, you know, took a few days for it to thicken up.

00:23:39.760 --> 00:23:46.940
 And, you know, eventually it did thicken up, uh, kind of as all raw milk does when you leave it out, you know, to ferment for a certain amount of time.

00:23:47.180 --> 00:23:53.680
 And then that thickened raw milk clabber, um, I took that and I used that as a starter culture for making yogurt.

00:23:53.900 --> 00:23:56.310
 And what do you know, the culture worked.

00:23:56.850 --> 00:24:07.240
 Um, and the more that I used that, you know, reuse that, that, that culture for making yogurt, the more active and, uh, the more active the culture became, the faster it fermented the yogurt.

00:24:07.460 --> 00:24:11.640
 And the better the flavor developed and the better the texture of the yogurt got over time.

00:24:12.200 --> 00:24:23.920
 And, uh, it was really beautiful to see that you could just invoke from the, you know, the raw milk, the appropriate cultures that could help develop just the right textures and flavors that you're looking for in yogurt.

00:24:24.180 --> 00:24:32.220
 And with more and more experimenting, I realized that you could do the same thing with kefir culture that kefir had, it has, it has, you know, very similar microorganisms to raw milk.

00:24:32.220 --> 00:24:38.480
 And if you use kefir as a starter culture for making yogurt, the yogurt will thicken up, you know, very differently than kefir.

00:24:38.480 --> 00:24:49.770
 It will taste very differently too, because of the different conditions you create by cooking the milk first and then incubating that, uh, you know, inoculated, cooked inoculated milk at 110 degrees Fahrenheit for several hours.

00:24:49.850 --> 00:24:54.290
 That changes the ferment and, uh, the cultures in kefir adapt.

00:24:54.310 --> 00:24:58.520
 To the cultures of the higher temperature fermentation and end up turning into yogurt.

00:24:58.580 --> 00:25:08.010
 And the more you, the more generations of yogurt you make, the more, uh, those cultures become adapted to the higher temperature fermentation and the more stable that culture becomes.

00:25:08.310 --> 00:25:09.460
 Wow. Yeah.

00:25:09.460 --> 00:25:15.930
 I've always made it using commercial yogurt and figuring that I would get St. Benoit, one that tastes really, tastes really good.

00:25:16.230 --> 00:25:21.830
 But yeah, I did notice that it always, after a couple of different times, it wouldn't, it wouldn't set into it.

00:25:21.830 --> 00:25:22.450
 It wouldn't turn out the same.

00:25:22.570 --> 00:25:28.670
 Yeah, those, the, the freeze dried package cultures that cheesemakers buy or that are found in, you know, commercial yogurts that people use.

00:25:28.750 --> 00:25:30.390
 The starter cultures are non-profitable.

00:25:30.510 --> 00:25:38.150
 They cannot keep, they don't contain a diversity of microorganisms in them that's resilient and resistant to unwanted, uh, cultures finding their way in.

00:25:38.150 --> 00:25:47.230
 Whereas traditional cultures were diverse, uh, contain many different cultures in them that prevented the growth of, um, uh, unwanted, uh, cultures that could have spoiled the batch.

00:25:48.360 --> 00:25:51.260
 How long has, um, has Salt Spring Island been your home?

00:25:52.090 --> 00:25:53.070
 Uh, not very long.

00:25:53.150 --> 00:25:55.970
 I actually just moved there, uh, to write this book.

00:25:56.580 --> 00:26:05.000
 Uh, prior to that, I was living on another island, uh, just, uh, just nearby called Main Island, which is a really minor island, a really small island.

00:26:05.000 --> 00:26:06.180
 There's just a thousand people on it.

00:26:06.700 --> 00:26:15.480
 Um, I went to Main Island to pursue a farming apprenticeship and ended up, uh, uh, uh, ended up staying there after the apprenticeship.

00:26:15.640 --> 00:26:19.520
 I found it to be a really welcoming and, uh, supportive community for organic farming.

00:26:20.040 --> 00:26:26.660
 And, uh, the farmer under whom I apprenticed, uh, gave me a wonderful opportunity to manage his farm for a number of years.

00:26:26.780 --> 00:26:40.850
 And so I stayed around on Main Island for a while, um, yeah, farmed there on a small scale, you know, growing, uh, produce for, for market, keeping goats and a sort of goat cooperative with a number of other young folks on the island.

00:26:40.850 --> 00:26:49.290
 Um, and, uh, using that milk for making cheese, um, using the goat's manure, of course, for growing, uh, gorgeous organic vegetables.

00:26:49.890 --> 00:27:03.420
 Um, but, um, when I got the, um, when I got the contract to write this book, um, uh, you know, I realized that I didn't have access to, you know, enough milk on the island to make the cheeses that I wanted to make for the, for the book.

00:27:03.500 --> 00:27:10.890
 The only animals, um, the only milking animals on the island were, you know, the few goats that we were keeping and it just wasn't enough.

00:27:11.110 --> 00:27:27.670
 And so I made the move over to neighboring Salt Spring in part, um, um, um, to have access to better milk, uh, to have access to cow's milk and goat's milk and sheep's milk that I needed, uh, to experiment with and, you know, prepare cheeses for the book, uh, to be photographed.

00:27:28.570 --> 00:27:35.890
 Um, and, um, and, uh, Salt Spring Island has been pretty good to me.

00:27:36.810 --> 00:27:42.090
 So we met you through Molly and Zach, who Chelsea just recently interviewed for this podcast also.

00:27:42.090 --> 00:27:45.690
 Um, and, uh, were you using their milk?

00:27:45.890 --> 00:27:46.350
 That's right.

00:27:46.530 --> 00:27:54.450
 So I've, I've been involved in the, in the, in the Bullock Lake farming community, um, which is a, you know, group of young farmers that kind of collaborate together.

00:27:54.610 --> 00:27:56.550
 Molly and Zach are the farm keepers.

00:27:56.770 --> 00:28:07.020
 Um, but they don't, uh, you know, they, they encourage everyone who's interested in contributing to collaborate on their vision, uh, what their, of what their farm means.

00:28:07.540 --> 00:28:11.400
 Um, and, uh, there's all sorts of people that get involved in all sorts of interesting ways.

00:28:11.400 --> 00:28:18.360
 And they had, uh, a friend, uh, a neighbor, uh, who was keeping her, her Jersey cows there.

00:28:18.840 --> 00:28:29.700
 Um, and, uh, uh, she was milking the cows on the farm and I was able to get access to that wonderful, uh, raw milk that I used for many of my cheese making experiments.

00:28:29.700 --> 00:28:42.260
 And to prepare a lot of the cheeses in the book, uh, the, you know, the, the community there has been very supportive of what I do and, um, uh, you know, uh, really encouraging.

00:28:42.660 --> 00:28:51.200
 And, you know, a lot of the, you know, even the, the photographs of the book were taken in Molly and Zach's kitchen, um, because they open, you know, the world up to so much.

00:28:51.200 --> 00:28:58.620
 And I've been, you know, very grateful, um, you know, without them, you know, much of the book as it is would not have been possible.

00:28:58.620 --> 00:29:04.230
 Awesome. Yeah. We went, we went last summer and it was totally beautiful place.

00:29:04.730 --> 00:29:21.330
 Um, and this feeling that like you're on an Island. I mean, I grew up on an Island, but it was very close to Seattle, but this is like, you're on an Island or so there's, everybody seems to be doing, playing with how you can, uh, this self-sufficiency and community sufficiency.

00:29:21.330 --> 00:29:29.210
 Yeah. The Island, the Island mentality instills in people an interest in, in cultivating their community and, and being responsible for themselves and their neighbors.

00:29:29.610 --> 00:29:39.400
 Uh, in particular with food, people see, uh, very clearly where their food comes from on Island, either it's produced on the Island or comes from off Island.

00:29:39.500 --> 00:29:45.780
 And there's a very distinct difference in quality, um, from that, which is produced on the Island and that, which comes in from afar.

00:29:46.280 --> 00:29:57.220
 Um, and, uh, a very distinct difference in, you know, the, where your money goes to when you purchase vegetables at the supermarket compared to when you buy, you know, much nicer vegetables at a farmer's market.

00:29:57.220 --> 00:30:01.920
 You know, you're, you're, you're, you're, you're doing business with your neighbor and it's really, really, uh, wonderful.

00:30:02.640 --> 00:30:11.020
 And, um, uh, it seems as though almost all the islands in the sort of Salish Sea where I farm have a very similar mentality and community.

00:30:11.200 --> 00:30:19.370
 And for the most part, people are very supportive of the local farmers and treat them as heroes because they really are feeding their communities in a way that the, the fairies cannot.

00:30:20.070 --> 00:30:21.050
 Awesome.

