 And I'm interested in farming in community, like joining, you know, the folks at Bullock Lake Farm would be a dream.

00:32:51.530 --> 00:32:55.360
 Um, uh, I realized I can't, I can't do everything myself.

00:32:55.700 --> 00:33:01.580
 Um, you know, I've got certain strengths and other people have others and it's really, I've had my best farming experiences farming with others.

00:33:01.640 --> 00:33:06.040
 So I don't really want to invest in my own land and, you know, farm and make cheese there.

00:33:06.100 --> 00:33:07.080
 That's not what I want to do.

00:33:07.580 --> 00:33:12.650
 Like cheese making is just, you know, a small part of my, my, my, my farming interest.

00:33:12.650 --> 00:33:15.970
 It's, you know, my cheese making kind of fits into my full circle.

00:33:16.090 --> 00:33:16.970
 Cheese making philosophy.

00:33:17.170 --> 00:33:20.250
 I'm a serious gardener and I really into keeping livestock.

00:33:20.890 --> 00:33:23.290
 Um, I'm really into cooking and such.

00:33:23.330 --> 00:33:29.210
 And I don't really want to focus full time on cheese making like lots of folks ask me if I'm going to start up a cheese factory.

00:33:29.330 --> 00:33:32.030
 I'm like, no, that's just not my, that's just not my thing.

00:33:32.110 --> 00:33:34.310
 I don't want to sell my soul to sell my cheeses.

00:33:34.810 --> 00:33:45.980
 You know, if I, if I, if I, if I were to invest in a commercial facility to making cheeses, uh, the, you know, the amount of capital I'd have to put in, in order to create a facility that's up to the standards of production.

00:33:45.980 --> 00:33:47.680
 It would allow me to sell to my community.

00:33:48.200 --> 00:34:01.830
 Um, uh, unfortunately that like the loans that I would have to take, uh, in order to produce that facility, which would be in the several hundred, you know, thousand dollar range would, um, indenture me to selling my cheeses across Canada.

00:34:01.830 --> 00:34:04.210
 Like I wouldn't be able to just feed my community cheese.

00:34:04.290 --> 00:34:06.650
 I'd have to sell my cheese across the country.

00:34:06.650 --> 00:34:08.510
 And it just doesn't make sense to me.

00:34:09.250 --> 00:34:10.650
 Uh, I want to keep it small.

00:34:10.790 --> 00:34:15.290
 I want to keep it cottage, but unfortunately rules and regulations don't allow that sort of thing in Canada.

00:34:15.470 --> 00:34:16.870
 It's, you know, different places they do.

00:34:16.970 --> 00:34:23.770
 Apparently in Pennsylvania, you can make cheese and cottage scale and sell it at the farm stand, which is great, but we can't where we are.

00:34:24.250 --> 00:34:33.500
 Uh, someday, you know, I'd really love to open up a, uh, a cheesemaking school where people can come and learn with me and get a more involved learning experience.

00:34:33.500 --> 00:34:33.740
 Sure.

00:34:33.800 --> 00:34:39.220
 I love taking, you know, this class on the road and bringing it to people that aren't able to travel, you know, farm.

00:34:39.240 --> 00:34:45.120
 Farmers and such who are stuck with their livestock because they're the people who would make the most of this, uh, of these teachings.

00:34:45.630 --> 00:34:57.090
 But, uh, a lot of people would also get a lot of, a lot better of an experience if they came to, you know, my teach, my school where there would be, uh, you know, cheeses in various stages of ripening and development.

00:34:57.090 --> 00:35:05.570
 So they could see, you know, the process firsthand and see how the cheese is transformed because in a day long class, we can only talk about the changes that a cheese goes through.

00:35:05.570 --> 00:35:09.450
 But observing them firsthand would have a very different effect upon the learning experience.

