 I was just thinking about it.

00:35:21.850 --> 00:35:30.520
 It's been, it's been, it's been a while since I started making Sour Crowd inspired by Sandy Katz, who wrote the introduction to your book.

00:35:30.520 --> 00:35:36.310
 And, and that's, it seems to me like almost everyone, maybe there, maybe there's a thawing going on in our session.

00:35:36.590 --> 00:35:39.350
 It's still, I mean, it's still not, it's not universal yet.

00:35:39.350 --> 00:35:45.630
 Like a lot of people still aren't familiar with, with Sandor Katz and his revolutionary, um, fermentary ideas.

00:35:46.220 --> 00:35:57.880
 Um, a lot of people that come to my classes though, no, um, they are familiar with the principles of natural fermentation and, uh, they, you know, keep sauerkraut.

00:35:57.880 --> 00:36:08.410
 Uh, they make sauerkraut at home, they keep sourdough starter cultures at home and, you know, they've started making cheese and have wondered about, you know, more traditional methods, but they, you know, haven't been able to find resources just like me when I got started.

00:36:08.970 --> 00:36:14.950
 And, um, uh, they are, have been very eager to learn methods like this, but haven't known where to go.

00:36:14.950 --> 00:36:17.910
 And, uh, they're excited that these, these ideas are out there.

00:36:18.030 --> 00:36:22.450
 So there's a lot of people that come out to classes like that, who just wondered all this time, how, how to do it.

00:36:22.450 --> 00:36:27.550
 And, you know, made cheese according to the standard way, just like, like, just like you tried, you know, with package starter cultures.

00:36:27.550 --> 00:36:32.490
 But we're kind of gave up on the idea because they didn't want to do it that way, but just didn't know how to do it otherwise.

00:36:32.910 --> 00:36:35.370
 And so they've been, you know, kind of empowered by the workshop.

00:36:35.590 --> 00:36:38.330
 But that's kind of a small number of people that come to the classes.

00:36:38.470 --> 00:36:43.500
 Most people who come don't have experience with any cheesemaking whatsoever.

00:36:43.500 --> 00:36:52.250
 Like most of the people that came to the class today are just, um, you know, when I asked how many people have made yogurt, rather fermented dairy products in the class, only a few people put up their hands.

00:36:53.190 --> 00:37:01.800
 Um, by and large, it seems as though people are interested in, in, in, in, in having more of a role in feeding themselves and their families and their communities.

00:37:01.800 --> 00:37:15.340
 And, you know, not necessarily just the fermenters, but folks in general are excited about a transformation of, of, uh, uh, they're interested in what, you know, makes feeding possible.

00:37:15.340 --> 00:37:17.720
 They're interested in, in, in learning more about that process.

00:37:17.720 --> 00:37:22.620
 And they're naturally drawn to the sort of workshops that I offer because they're, they're really empowering and encouraging.

00:37:23.410 --> 00:37:23.650
 Awesome.

00:37:23.730 --> 00:37:35.390
 I thought it was too, like one thing, I mean, I'll definitely make some cheese at some point, but one thing that I know that I'll probably do all the time is that my mind was blown by the process of making cultured butter.

00:37:35.730 --> 00:37:37.510
 It was, it was so good.

00:37:37.550 --> 00:37:38.430
 It was so delicious.

00:37:39.230 --> 00:37:40.940
 And it looked fun to make.

00:37:40.940 --> 00:37:43.660
 So I should, I guess I could explain the process to your listeners.

00:37:44.240 --> 00:37:51.780
 Um, now the process of making cultured butter is, uh, uh, is probably the, the, the most beautiful expression of kefir's possibilities.

00:37:52.280 --> 00:37:57.080
 Uh, and it shows how appropriate a culture it is for making all these fermented dairy products.

00:37:57.240 --> 00:38:06.720
 And when I make my cultured butter, I take my kefir grains, my kefir culture, and I put them into a full fat cream, just a natural, preferably unhomogenized, no additive, uh, cream.

00:38:06.720 --> 00:38:12.360
 You know, the best of course is, you know, raw cream skimmed off of, uh, like a pastured animal's milk.

00:38:13.370 --> 00:38:19.950
 Um, you take that cream, you add the culture to it and you let it ferment at room temperature for about 24 hours.

00:38:20.630 --> 00:38:29.650
 And, uh, the kefir culture naturally thickens up that cream into cultured cream, uh, as the French call it, creme fraiche, uh, in about a day.

00:38:29.710 --> 00:38:34.060
 And that thickened creme fraiche is, is delicious and amazing just on its own.

00:38:34.320 --> 00:38:39.380
 Um, uh, you can eat that up by the spoonful as an alternative to, uh, to whipped cream.

00:38:39.380 --> 00:38:42.060
 It's like great on strawberries and baked goods of all sorts.

00:38:42.600 --> 00:38:48.800
 Um, but that creme fraiche can also then be churned into a beautiful cultured butter.

00:38:49.140 --> 00:38:55.820
 And this cultured butter is kind of different than, uh, you know, the sweet cream butter that most of us in North America are accustomed to.

00:38:56.240 --> 00:39:02.740
 A cultured butter is a more European style, uh, a butter that's made by first culturing the cream and then churning that cultured cream.

00:39:03.100 --> 00:39:10.480
 Um, and, uh, kefir can be used as a starter culture for making that, you know, cultured cream that can then be churned into a sort of kefir cultured butter.

00:39:11.180 --> 00:39:17.960
 Um, and the culturing process has all of these beneficial effects upon the butter that's made from this process.

00:39:18.440 --> 00:39:29.420
 Uh, uh, cultured butter ends up, you know, tasting better than sweet cream butter because the fermentation of the cream brings out all these interesting flavors, uh, that people didn't realize could come out in their butter.

00:39:29.420 --> 00:39:39.700
 They're not necessarily cheesy flavors, but all these complex, um, notes that just seem to come out of, seem to come out of this, um, out of the, out of the butter after it's, after it's made.

00:39:40.830 --> 00:39:48.170
 Um, culturing the cream before you transform into the butter also allows the butter to last longer.

00:39:48.270 --> 00:39:54.410
 The beneficial microbes of the culturing, um, protect the butter from becoming contaminated from unwanted microbes.

00:39:54.410 --> 00:39:58.130
 So the cultured butter actually lasts longer than, uh, uncultured butter.

00:39:59.220 --> 00:40:04.840
 Um, uh, cultured butter ends up having a higher fat content than ultra, than uncultured butter.

00:40:04.840 --> 00:40:24.900
 It's actually, um, uh, it's actually more buttery than sweet cream butter, um, because the, the culturing of the cream helps to break down the butterfat particles, um, prior to churning and allows the butterfat to come together, uh, even faster and firmer, um, and forms a more buttery butter.

00:40:25.040 --> 00:40:30.680
 And the final advantage of culturing the cream before you churn, which is something that's, is rarely, rarely done in North America.

00:40:30.680 --> 00:40:39.920
 It's really common in Europe, um, is that, uh, culturing the cream first breaks down the butterfat particles and allows the butterfat to come together into butter much more quickly.

00:40:40.020 --> 00:40:47.700
 And so typically it might take, you know, 25 minutes to an hour to hand churn sweet cream into butter.

00:40:47.700 --> 00:40:53.900
 If you ferment that cultured butter first, it may only take two minutes for you to churn that cultured cream into butter.

00:40:54.420 --> 00:41:08.800
 And, um, for people who have, you know, never seen this transformation happen before, um, doing it by hand, who have just done it in a blender, um, when it takes, you know, five minutes in the blender, it's amazing that, um, this butter can be churned out of the cream in less than a minute.

00:41:08.800 --> 00:41:22.980
 And as it happened in class today, you know, we passed the, the, the, the container around the class and, you know, before it made the whole way around, it separated into cultured butter and the cultured butter, um, was a beautiful creamy color because the animals that produced it were, were grass fed.

00:41:22.980 --> 00:41:31.180
 And the butter was extra flavorful because it was cultured, but it also, after it was churned, still had the kefir grains in it that we had added to the cream when we started the process.

00:41:31.660 --> 00:41:47.480
 And, um, when you then, uh, you know, you, you take that butter, you strain it of its buttermilk and the cultured buttermilk is delicious and, you know, totally different product than the fake cultured buttermilk you buy in grocery stores today, which is just skim milk cultured with, with, with lactic freeze-dried cultures.

00:41:48.060 --> 00:41:57.960
 Um, you take that butter, you strain it of its buttermilk, and then you take that butter, it's like filled with the kefir grains in it, um, and you put it, uh, into water to wash it.

00:41:57.960 --> 00:42:10.970
 And as you're washing the, the butter, which is a natural part of the butter making process to get the remainder of the buttermilk out, the kefir grains just fall out of the butter, um, and end up falling to the bottom of the cold washing water.

00:42:11.370 --> 00:42:17.390
 Um, whereas the butter rises to the top, um, and the butter that you make ends up totally free of kefir grains.

00:42:17.390 --> 00:42:26.470
 And then you can just go into the bottom of that washing water and pick up your, your pile of grains and then take them out and use them to put into more cream to make more cultured butter, keeping that process alive.

00:42:26.590 --> 00:42:28.730
 Yeah, it was, it was absolutely delicious.

00:42:28.850 --> 00:42:35.770
 And I know that the, the, the real buttermilk is, is pure magic for things like pancakes and waffles.

00:42:35.770 --> 00:42:42.200
 Yes, it's a beautiful ingredient to have in a home and, uh, almost worth making butter just for the buttermilk.

00:42:42.740 --> 00:42:43.000
 Awesome.

00:42:43.100 --> 00:42:44.720
 Yeah, I'll, I'll definitely be making that.

